edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186521238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521412 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ae34d415-ccc8-5608-abcb-26fa7f2fa29a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521423 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521423 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521423 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521423 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521423 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521423 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521423 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521423 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186521228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521423 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bddec77b-4bb5-5e2b-92e1-a0fa38b99c3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521430 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521430 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521430 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186521200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521430 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d0c989b5-7b3b-504b-8868-67605b31a160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521436 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521436 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521436 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521436 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521436 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521436 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521436 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521436 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186521310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521436 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "da0cc54c-5334-583d-91ac-093122073d60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521463 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521463 #edb/ref 1069 "q-59899" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521463 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521463 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6e/6e12ca457954f93778b772f2d0bd5a1e63e7e8e7eaf626d309300e97b23ab525.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of hexagons to rhombuses is $\\boxed{}:1$ because there are $\\boxed{}$ hexagons for every $1$ rhombus." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521463 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "162ad66e-4fa5-5917-937f-4dfd0e95e3fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521463 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we count the number of hexagons. We see that there are $4$ hexagons.\n\nThen, we count the number of rhombuses. We see that there are $2$ rhombuses.\n\nSo the ratio of hexagons to rhombuses is $4:2.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,4 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,2 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $2:1$ is equivalent to $4:2.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of hexagons to rhombuses is $2:1$ because there are $2$ hexagons for every $1$ rhombus.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521470 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521470 #edb/ref 1069 "q-92941" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521470 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521470 #edb/ref 1071 "What is missing in the following sentence?\n\nIn order to make some flannels, Martha bought $5$ meters of cotton fabric and $4$ meters of linen fabric, so there are $\\boxed{}$ meters of cotton fabric for each meter of linen fabric." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521470 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2668924b-c4c3-5180-8799-da87f8fa9aec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521470 #edb/ref 1074 "The ratio of the amount of cotton fabric to the amount of linen fabric is $5:4.$ \n\nWe can rewrite this ratio as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nThis means that there are $\\frac{5}{4}$ meters of cotton fabric for each meter of linen fabric." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521487 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521487 #edb/ref 1069 "q-171123" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521487 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521487 #edb/ref 1071 "$(5.2)\\cdot (-3)=${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521487 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e520184-edf6-56d7-ae3f-333c3748146f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521487 #edb/ref 1074 "We have a product of a positive number $(5.2)$ and a negative number $(-3).$ So the product will be negative:\n\n$$\n5.2\\cdot (-3)=-\\,\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply $5.2$ by $3$ without any signs: $5.2\\cdot 3=15.6.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n5.2\\cdot (-3)=-15.6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521488 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521488 #edb/ref 1069 "q-350258" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521488 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521488 #edb/ref 1071 "Ethan and Lucas are swimmers. In the previous season, $75%$ of Ethan’s races were freestyle, while the decimal proportion of Lucas’s races that were freestyle was $0.4.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Less than half of Ethan's races were freestyle.\n\nII. More than half of Lucas's races were freestyle.\n\nIII. Ethan had a greater percentage of freestyle races compared to Lucas." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521488 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e597010-3568-5f92-b7bf-8866f8549553" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521488 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n75%>50%,\n$$\n\nmore than half of Ethan's races were freestyle.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n0.4<0.5.\n$$\n\nSo, less than half of Lucas's races were freestyle.\n\n- Statement III is true. We can compare $75%$ and $0.4$ by converting the decimal into a percent. \n\nTo do this, we multiply it by $100:$\n\n$$\n0.4\\times 100=40%\n$$\n\nSince $75%>40%,$ Ethan had a **greater** percentage of freestyle races compared to Lucas.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521493 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521493 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521493 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521493 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/2d/2d4fc837b149453ef874950260279781e57ca398b92023e28ebe4fb3b841c311.png)\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing numbers in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of rectangles to kites is $2:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $\\,\\boxed{}\\,$ rectangles for every $5$ kites." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521493 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4be9a2f5-2f4a-5a28-99ac-bf9fd6c5ffaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521493 #edb/ref 1074 "We can express the ratio of the number of rectangles to the number of kites as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{number of rectangles}:\\text{number of kites}\n$$\n\nTo find this ratio, we start by counting the number of occurrences of each shape:\n\n- First, we count the number of rectangles. There are $2$ rectangles.\n\n- Next, we count the number of kites. There are $5$ kites.\n\nTherefore, the ratio of rectangles to kites is $2:5.$\n\nTherefore, the missing numbers are $5$ and $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521495 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521495 #edb/ref 1069 "q-223451" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521495 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521495 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $7?$\n\nI. $631$\n\nII. $782$\n\nIII. $686$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521495 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d3c9434-4ef2-5a17-95da-683191ed9ce8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521495 #edb/ref 1074 "To check if a number is divisible by $7,$ double the last digit, subtract the result from the remaining digits, and then check if the difference is divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $631,$ we have that \n\n$$\n63-2(1)=63-2=61\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $631$ is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $782,$ we have that \n\n$$\n78-2(2)=78-4=74\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $782$ is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $686,$ we have that \n\n$$\n68-2(6)=68-12=56\n$$\n\nwhich is divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $686$ is divisible by $7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521501 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521501 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99753" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521501 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521501 #edb/ref 1071 "What is $0÷(-\\frac{8}{21})?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521501 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "57848bee-3e72-5d15-a97c-92bd970ffedc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521501 #edb/ref 1074 "Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore, \n\n$$\n0÷(-\\frac{8}{21})=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521514 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521514 #edb/ref 1069 "q-5754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521514 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521514 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d4/d43d45e070fe27d03717c2c88740b4536a23c7adba0ba2aa2ae451ecab34dbb5.png)\n\nWhat are the $xy$-coordinates of the point shown in the axes above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521514 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "785b3e3d-2e78-5863-9118-440c52c1081d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521514 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the coordinates of the point, we inspect its position along the $x$ and $y$ axes.\n\nThe point is $5$ units to the *left* of the origin, in the *negative* direction of the $x$-axis. So, the point has an $x$-coordinate of $-5.$\n\nAdditionally, the point is $7$ units *below* the origin, in the *negative* direction of the $y$-axis. So, the point has a $y$-coordinate of $-7.$\n\nThe point has an $x$-coordinate of $-5$ and a $y$-coordinate of $-7,$ so its coordinates are $(-5,-7).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521562 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521562 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98374" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521562 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521562 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are natural numbers?\n\nI. $-\\frac{4}{3}$\n\nII. $3$\n\nIII. $-4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521562 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e58a84f8-02f0-5e14-9a24-64165fe53601" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521562 #edb/ref 1074 "The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers.\n\n$$\n1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…\n$$\n\nOf the given numbers, $3$ is a natural number, but $-\\frac{4}{3}$ and $-4$ are not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521563 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521563 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67032" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521563 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521563 #edb/ref 1071 "Translate the following phrase into an algebraic expression: \"Twenty more than a number.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521563 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e800ccd8-93c4-50ec-b967-b7f8e3af90fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521563 #edb/ref 1074 "If we denote our number by $a$, then the phrase \"twenty more than a number\" can be written as\n\n$$\na+20.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521578 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521578 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1256085e-bc62-52a3-92ec-b26d83a1910e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521578 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186521487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521587 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521587 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "32bbbec1-147c-531a-80e7-17ca41706811" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521587 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186521563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521589 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "349033cd-9a87-5bcb-b755-f9cf20d2c862" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521589 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186521463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521592 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521592 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3fa15d6e-8ca0-5b34-8c9c-5a695066d25f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521592 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186521514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521593 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521593 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "41349a7a-8278-5485-9fe4-4a16f70d8dab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521593 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186521562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521598 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521598 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "578f8bd4-8f1c-583e-8c9c-d9bf4101c230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521598 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521610 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521610 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6a06965e-66a0-5eb2-835d-4ff71fdad0dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521610 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186521470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521618 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521634 